          &lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/thead&gt;
  &lt;tbody&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vessel&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;Research Vessel Polarstern&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Expedition&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;PS156&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dates&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;6 August – 14 October 2026&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Days at sea&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;70&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Route&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;Tromsø – Greenland – Lomonosov Ridge - North Pole - Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge complex – Bremerhaven&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Institutions&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;Alfred Wegener Institute, University of Bergen and Tromsø, NORCE, MARUM, OGS&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;post&#34;&gt;Post&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here I am, once again in the Arctic. This time aboard the legendary Polarstern, as part of the Lomonosov-Alpha-Morris Jesup Rise Expedition 1 (LAMEX 1), led by 
.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We left Tromsø on August 6 and our return to Bremerhaven is scheduled on October 14; yeah, exactly 70 days at sea. The expedition also serves as a scientific platform for the 
 project. Check out the detailed scientific plan 
.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am part of the Geophysics group, working with the Seismic team. Seismic surveys have added another layer of fascination/mystery to these expeditions as they allow us to look beneath the Arctic seafloor and &amp;ldquo;see&amp;rdquo; the geometry of sediment layers, in some places reaching up to about 2 km below the seabed. These sediments may contain a record of the Arctic Ocean reaching back roughly 20 million years, and potentially even further. Trying to grasp these timescales is, quite honestly, difficult.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even more mysterious is that some sediment layers dating back 20 to 40 million years are unusually thin in some areas. Here, we are a bit like detectives, looking for where these layers are thicker and using these differences to understand why the same layers became much thinner in other parts of the Arctic Ocean.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This post comes with a small collection of moments from the expedition: deploying seismic instruments; preparing to fly by helicopter onto an ice floe near the Lomonosov Ridge to help with the deployment of an eXpendable Conductivity, Temperature and Depth (XCTD), using, surprisingly, a modified fishing rod; and, of course, spending time on a giant ice floe at the North Pole (that one could not be missed!). And then there is Polarstern, navigating through the ice in what sometimes feels like a lunar landscape. Listing these experiences in a few sentences does not really do them justice. Each one could probably need a chapter of its own.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is a privilege to be here (for the second time; read about the first time 
 and to contribute, even in a small way, to understanding one of the most remote and fascinating environments on Earth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A round of thanks to everyone who makes this possible: Alfred Wegener Institute, Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research for providing the infrastructure and means for polar research; i2B Into The Blue for its scientific partnership; the Polarstern crew (Reederei F. Laeisz) for safely carrying us through the Arctic Ocean and supporting the deployment of our instruments; Deutscher Wetterdienst for providing dedicated weather forecasts through a meteorologist on board; Northern Helicopter GmbH for the pilots and engineers who make our helicopter operations possible and safe; And, last but certainly not least, thanks to 
 for having me on board and for making me part of this adventure :)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Photo credits: black and white photos taken by Mario Hoppman, color photo in front of the helicopter taken by Estella Weigelt&lt;/p&gt;


